@@ -243,7 +243,7 @@ Rules:
 - `lane_target.py plan <projects.jsonl> --agent <id> --server <server> [--suffix /repo] [--prefix P]` emits the intended `bunker://<agent>/<home><suffix>` target per lane. The agent's home is **resolved from the agent**, never templated: homes are `/home/bunker-<full-id>`, so a `{agent}` template builds a wrong path that still parses.
 - `lane_target.py scan <projects.jsonl> [--allow-remote]` checks every row and exits nonzero if any target is invalid.
 - `scripts/node_freshness.py --agent A --server S [--path REL]` proves the **checkout** a node will work is the tree `origin/main` has: branch, HEAD, whether HEAD is an ancestor of `origin/main`, the lag count and the files that differ, plus every uncommitted path — gathered in ONE `bunker exec` round trip, with the agent's home resolved from the agent (never templated). `--workdir PATH` measures a local checkout with no bunker, no network and no database; `--json` emits the object layer 90 stores in the manifest. Exit codes: `0` PASS, `1` drift or dirty, `2` usage or unreachable — an unmeasurable node is not a PASS. A repo with no `origin` remote is a FAIL naming that, and the comparison ref falls back to `origin/HEAD` (named in the row) so a `trunk`-default repo is measured instead of mislabelled. `scripts/verify-node.sh` folds the verdict into `VERIFY:` (step 5; unmeasurable is WARN unless `--strict-freshness`).
-- `scripts/node_seed_drift.py --agent A --server S` (or `--home DIR`) proves the **seed** a node carries is still what its digest record pins: it runs `scripts/node_seed.py`'s own `check` — the seeder is staged to a temp path on the node and removed again inside ONE `bunker exec` round trip, because provisioning deletes the node-side copy after it applies — with the record the seed left (`$HOME/.hermes/seed-applied.json`, home `$HOME`), so the verdict is the instrument's own and the wrapper cannot drift from it. The PASS row counts the seeded files; the FAIL row names each drifted path with the expected and measured digest prefixes and each path absent on the node; the wrapper never reimplements the digest comparison. Exit codes: `0` matched, `1` drift (or an absent record under `--strict-seed`), `2` usage, `3` could not measure — a node still being provisioned has no seed record, a corrupt record is not a tampered copy, and "cannot measure" is not drift; both are WARN, and `--strict-seed` refuses the unknown. `scripts/verify-node.sh` folds it into `VERIFY:` as step 6: a drifted seed alone drives `VERIFY: FAIL`, an unmeasurable seed is WARN (FAIL under `--strict-seed`).
+- `scripts/node_seed_drift.py --agent A --server S` (or `--home DIR`) proves the **seed** a node carries is still what its digest record pins: it runs `scripts/node_seed.py`'s own `check` — the seeder is staged to a temp path on the node and removed again inside ONE `bunker exec` round trip, because provisioning deletes the node-side copy after it applies — with the record the seed left (`$HOME/.hermes/seed-applied.json`, home `$HOME`), so the verdict is the instrument's own and the wrapper cannot drift from it. The PASS row counts the seeded files and **only a count greater than zero can produce one**: a record whose `files` collection is empty — or absent, or not a list — is refused with `FAIL seed_drift — <record>: the record declares no files (<shape>) — an empty seed certifies nothing` and exit `1`, because nothing it declares could agree with anything on the node, while the seeder's own `check` prints the same `0 files present and matching` row and exits 0 for it (CF-379: `0 files present and matching` certified a node whose seed applied nothing). No reason field on the record rescues it: the probe exists to prove digests, and a record that names none proves nothing; a node that is meant to carry no seed should carry no seed record, which is the absent-record WARN below. The FAIL row names each drifted path with the expected and measured digest prefixes and each path absent on the node; the wrapper never reimplements the digest comparison. Exit codes: `0` matched, `1` drift (or a record that declares no files, or an absent record under `--strict-seed`), `2` usage, `3` could not measure — a node still being provisioned has no seed record, a corrupt record is not a tampered copy, and "cannot measure" is not drift; both are WARN, and `--strict-seed` refuses the unknown. `scripts/verify-node.sh` folds it into `VERIFY:` as step 6: a drifted seed alone drives `VERIFY: FAIL`, a record that declares no files drives `VERIFY: FAIL` the same way (the checker's exit `1` is already the `bad` branch — no new verb), and an unmeasurable seed is WARN (FAIL under `--strict-seed`).
 - Lane writers (`scripts/configure_family.py`) only `mkdir` a **local** target; a remote target is registered verbatim and materialised by the node side.
 
 ## Failure semantics
diff --git a/tests/selftest.sh b/tests/selftest.sh
index 4101aa6..400799d 100755
--- a/tests/selftest.sh
+++ b/tests/selftest.sh
@@ -4221,6 +4221,62 @@ else
   fails=$((fails+1)); printf '%s\n' "$out" | sed 's/^/    | /'
 fi
 
+# CF-379: a record that PARSES but declares no files. node_seed.py check prints `0 files present
+# and matching` and exits 0 for it, so the wrapper's PASS branch — "the seeder exited 0 with a
+# parseable count" — certified a node whose seed applied nothing. The record is not UNMEASURABLE:
+# it was read and it is conclusively empty, so the verdict is a named FAIL on the empty collection.
+printf '{"files": []}' > "$vn_rec"
+out=$(vn_run "$vn/tree"); rc=$?
+if [ "$rc" -eq 1 ] \
+  && printf '%s\n' "$out" | grep -Fxq 'VERIFY: FAIL (1)' \
+  && printf '%s\n' "$out" | grep -Fq '  FAIL  seed_drift — ' \
+  && printf '%s\n' "$out" | grep -Fq 'the record declares no files' \
+  && printf '%s\n' "$out" | grep -Fq 'the files collection is empty' \
+  && ! printf '%s\n' "$out" | grep -Fxq 'VERIFY: PASS'; then
+  say "  PASS  a seed record that declares no files -> exit $rc, VERIFY: FAIL naming the empty collection (not a PASS)"
+else
+  say "  FAIL  an empty seed record was not a named FAIL (exit $rc): $(printf '%s\n' "$out" | grep -E '^VERIFY|seed_drift' | head -3 | tr '\n' ' ')"
+  fails=$((fails+1)); printf '%s\n' "$out" | sed 's/^/    | /'
+fi
+
+# The CF-379 control: the SAME empty record through the checker at 226bae2 — the row's measured
+# premise commit — inside the SAME verifier must still reach VERIFY: PASS and print `0 seeded files
+# present and matching`. Without it the arm above could be green on a fixture that refuses every
+# seed. Spliced from the pre-fix revision, not hand-written: `declared_files()` does not exist there,
+# so there is no refusal to weaken and the control cannot drift into a stub of the fix.
+vn_pfx="$vn/prefix-cf379/scripts"
+mkdir -p "$vn_pfx"
+cp -r "$vn/tree/scripts/." "$vn_pfx/"
+if git -C "$HERE" show 226bae2:scripts/node_seed_drift.py > "$vn_pfx/node_seed_drift.py" 2>/dev/null; then
+  if cmp -s "$vn_pfx/node_seed_drift.py" "$vn/tree/scripts/node_seed_drift.py"; then
+    say "  FAIL  the CF-379 control is not a pre-fix checker — byte-identical to the tree under test"; fails=$((fails+1))
+  else
+    out=$(vn_run "$vn/prefix-cf379"); rc=$?
+    if [ "$rc" -eq 0 ] && printf '%s\n' "$out" | grep -Fxq 'VERIFY: PASS' \
+      && printf '%s\n' "$out" | grep -Fq '0 seeded files present and matching'; then
+      say "  PASS  pre-fix 226bae2 certifies the same empty record: VERIFY: PASS, exit $rc (the fixture detects CF-379)"
+    else
+      say "  FAIL  pre-fix 226bae2 control did not pass the empty record (exit $rc)"; fails=$((fails+1))
+      printf '%s\n' "$out" | sed 's/^/    | /'
+    fi
+  fi
+else
+  say "  FAIL  pre-fix source 226bae2:scripts/node_seed_drift.py unavailable"; fails=$((fails+1))
+fi
+
+# Green control for the two arms above: the record restored — its two seeded files still match —
+# must reach VERIFY: PASS again, naming a count greater than zero. A red that cannot go green
+# proves nothing, and this is the count a pass is supposed to look like.
+cp "$vn/good-record.json" "$vn_rec"
+out=$(vn_run "$vn/tree"); rc=$?
+if [ "$rc" -eq 0 ] && printf '%s\n' "$out" | grep -Fxq 'VERIFY: PASS' \
+  && printf '%s\n' "$out" | grep -Fq '2 seeded files present and matching'; then
+  say "  PASS  the record restored with its 2 seeded files -> VERIFY: PASS, exit $rc (a pass names a count above zero)"
+else
+  say "  FAIL  the restored two-file record did not verify (exit $rc)"; fails=$((fails+1))
+  printf '%s\n' "$out" | sed 's/^/    | /'
+fi
+
 # absent record: no seed was ever applied — unmeasured, so the gate refuses to call it verified.
 mv "$vn_rec" "$vn/record.bak"
 out=$(vn_run "$vn/tree"); rc=$?
